A||||6||fotofrank||A little head work||||5||
Z||000000||fotofrank||04-14-2001||03:46 PM||||I am going to get my "extra" head reworked for my '84 Duke. Any reason to change lifters ( go to roller) or rockers being changed to roller type? Is a mill of 0.005 off of the head realistic for power gain? What have some of you Iron Duke devoties done? <P>------------------<BR>Peace and Happiness<BR>Frank and his fotoflash, an '84 SE 2M4<BR>http://www.homestead.com/fotofrank/foto1.html||||reg||5
Z||000001||85Josh||04-14-2001||05:29 PM||||Frank, .005 might not even clean up all the warpage, and there is also the possibility that the combustion chambers aren't the same depth, so you may need to cut more than that. Someone on this forum has a computer dyno that can tell you how much your compession ratio will go up, and how much that will increase power.<P>Roller rockers might free up a little power, but their main advantage is to allow the engine to rev higher. They also reduce side pressure on the valve stem, thus reducing valve stem wear.<P>------------------<BR>85 2M4(Lil' Bit)||||reg||1
Z||000002||Taijiguy||04-15-2001||09:54 AM||||I have Desktop Dyno, if I have all the information (and I mean ALL) I can give you an estimate of compression/horsepower gains<BR>~Taiji||||reg||1
Z||000003||88formula||04-15-2001||12:24 PM|||| I ported the heads on my brothers Iron Duke and milled the heads about .010" The engine lost a little low end grunt because I removed to much from the intake track but it does pull harder in the upper rpm band than it ever did before. If I could port the heads again I would have just smoothed out the bowl area and left the rest alone. That way he would have retained the low-end torque he had before. Milling .005” from the heads won’t make much difference in the compression ratio. <P>While you have the heads off make sure you dull any sharp edges in the combustion chambers to help ward off detonation. Have a good machine shop do a performance multi angle valve job on the heads. <BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||MrPBody||04-15-2001||09:42 PM||||I did a mild port job on my '85 SE, with no modifications to the combustion chamber other than smoothing sharp edges.  See  <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/008608.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/008608.html</A> .  Subjectively, I feel a little improvement at high revs with no loss of low-end.  All undocumented, of course.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I think the 2.5's combustion chamber is sufficiently steep and deep that milling 0.005" -- or whatever it needs to true the mating surface -- will have minimal effect on compression.  Just my shoot-from-the-hip guess, though.<P>I would not recommend modifying the combustion chamber to un-shroud the intake valve.  I think the chamber shape is designed to induce intake swirl, and modifying that shape may trash low-end performance and economy.<P>Roller tappets would require a different cam, and maybe block modifications for lifter retainers.  If you're lucky, maybe your engine has been replaced and you already have rollers.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||NSUMEY||04-16-2001||09:46 AM||||carefull changing lifters!!! pushrods size, valve and spring hieght and cam interchangability is a harrowing experience!! I know...going through it now!!||||reg||4
